WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.581
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:06.581 --> 00:00:08.039
ISAAC METCALF: In
this video, we'll

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:11.430
be discussing Goodie Bag
7, or crystalline defects.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.980
The objectives are to
visualize zero dimensional, one

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.750
dimensional, and two
dimensional defects

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.640
in crystalline materials.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.860
You'll need a double-sided
strip of adhesive,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.150
two Plexiglas panels, and
500 1/16th-inch metal beads.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.920
First, pull off
the protective film

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.260
on the top of your
acrylic sheet.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.660
Take four pieces of
double-sided tape

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.650
and put one piece on each of
the edges of the acrylic sheet.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.120
Then carefully put your balls
in the middle of the square made

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.070
by the four pieces of tape.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.550
Finally remove the film on the
top of the other acrylic sheet

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.350
and put it on top.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.870
When you're finished, you
should have a completed defect

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.362
modeling kit.

00:00:50.362 --> 00:00:52.320
CAROLYN JONS: Now that
you've built your model,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.170
let's use it to visualize how
material processing impacts

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.780
the number of defects
visible in a crystal.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.370
When looking at our
original crystal,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.020
you can see several defects.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.160
These are a vacancy,
line defect,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.220
and grain boundary defect.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.670
Material processing can lead
to both increases and decreases

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.890
in the number of
defects in the material.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.590
A process that leads to an
increased number of defects

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.470
is extrusion or rolling,
because when we extrude or roll

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.650
a material, we force the atoms
to go into certain places

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.780
and those aren't necessarily
the most energetically

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.380
favorable states.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.020
Let's look at our
model to understand

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.980
how material processing leads to
an increased number of defects.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.440
When we shake the model,
we're able to add defects

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.712
to the system.

00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:34.920
As you can see now, our
model has an increased number

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.610
of vacancies, line defects,
and grain boundary defects.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.260
Other material
processing methods

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.750
allow for a decrease in
the number of vacancies.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.700
One possibility for
this is annealing.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.800
When we anneal, we
heat the material up,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.470
and then we cool it slowly and
allow the atoms to move back

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.840
to their more favorable states.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.030
To illustrate annealing,
let's tap gently

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.010
on the side of our model.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.410
As you can see, the number
of defects in our material

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.510
has been substantially reduced.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.660
In this Goodie Bag, we
were able to visualize

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.830
some of the defects
seen in many crystals,

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.090
and we were also able to see
how processing conditions impact

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.749
the number of
defects in a crystal.

